



Anatomy of a Basic JUnit Test

In standard Java projects (using Maven or Gradle), tests live in the src/test/java directory, 
mirroring the package structure of your main code in src/main/java.

Example:

public class Calculator {
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

A unit test class (CalculatorTest.java) typically follows the Arrange-Act-Assert (or Given-When-Then) pattern:

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class CalculatorTest {

    @Test
    void testAddTwoNumbers() {
        // 1. Arrange: Set up the object and test inputs
        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();

        // 2. Act: Call the method being tested
        int result = calculator.add(2, 3);

        // 3. Assert: Verify the output matches expectations
        assertEquals(5, result, "2 + 3 should equal 5");
    }
}


Key JUnit Annotations & Assertions

JUnit relies on annotations to manage the test lifecycle and assertion methods to validate outcomes.
• @Test. Marks a method as an executable test case.
• @BeforeEach / @AfterEach. Runs setup or cleanup code before and after every individual @Test method in the class.
• @BeforeAll / @AfterAll. Runs one-time setup or teardown logic once for the entire test class.
• assertEquals(expected, actual). Checks that two values are equal.
• assertTrue(condition) / assertFalse(condition). Verifies boolean outcomes.
• assertThrows(Exception.class, () -> ...). Confirms that a method throws the expected exception under invalid conditions.


Best Practices for Effective Unit Tests

• Test in isolation. Mock external services, databases, or network calls using Mockito so your tests run quickly and only fail when the specific unit's logic breaks.
• Cover edge cases. Write separate test methods for valid inputs, boundary values, null inputs, and expected error conditions.
• Keep tests independent. Ensure tests do not rely on the execution order or shared state left behind by other tests.
• Automate execution. Integrate tests into your build tools (Maven/Gradle) and CI/CD pipelines so they run automatically on every code commit.
